WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.102
-Our next guest is an Emmy-
and Oscar-nominated actress

00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:05.071
you know from movies like
"Do the Right Thing,"

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:06.739
"Fearless," and
"White Men Can't Jump,"

00:00:06.739 --> 00:00:09.476
as well as the HBO Max series
"The Flight Attendant."

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:11.710
She stars in the second season
of "Your Honor."

00:00:11.710 --> 00:00:15.314
New episodes debut Fridays
on streaming and on demand

00:00:15.314 --> 00:00:16.883
and air Sunday nights
on Showtime.

00:00:16.883 --> 00:00:18.318
Let's take a look.

00:00:18.318 --> 00:00:21.754
-Have I given you the impression
that any of this is optional?

00:00:21.754 --> 00:00:23.990
-I don't know why you're
trying to push me?

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:25.791
-Seriously?
Everything you know

00:00:25.791 --> 00:00:28.527
about the Baxters, everything
you've seen them do, it --

00:00:28.527 --> 00:00:32.765
honestly, it's just driving me
a little bat-[Bleep] crazy

00:00:32.765 --> 00:00:35.368
that I have to persuade you
to take them down.

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:36.802
-Ah.

00:00:36.802 --> 00:00:40.440
-Please welcome back to the show
Rosie Perez, everybody!

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.108
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:43.108 --> 00:00:46.112
[ Mid-tempo music plays ]

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:52.318
♪♪

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:58.491
♪♪

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:01.227
Welcome back to the show!
How are you?

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:04.130
-I'm good.
I'm only wearing one Spanx,

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:05.365
so I'm good.
I can breathe.

00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:08.000
-When you -- So, at two Spanx,
you have trouble

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.503
with your just respiratory?
-Yes. Yes.

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:13.439
-Hey, so, you are --
you're a New Yorker.

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:15.641
-Yep.
-You're an actor,

00:01:15.641 --> 00:01:17.843
you're a choreographer,
you're an activist.

00:01:17.843 --> 00:01:20.813
-I was a choreographer.
-You was. You're no longer.

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:22.849
-I got arthritis now.
-Okay.

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:26.485
-In my knees, in my hips.
I got a broken toe.

00:01:26.485 --> 00:01:30.323
-Do you think that all came from
your young life as a dancer?

00:01:30.323 --> 00:01:32.124
-Absolutely.
I mean, you know,

00:01:32.124 --> 00:01:34.627
I was dancing hip-hop,
you know, on concrete.

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:36.129
-[ Laughs ]
-And then, when I was

00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:38.030
on "Soul Train,"
I'm dancing in high heels,

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.066
jumping up and down
like an idiot,

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:42.468
having the time of my life,
and now I'm paying for it.

00:01:42.468 --> 00:01:44.169
So yeah.
-I guess it's true,

00:01:44.169 --> 00:01:45.471
what you're putting yourself
through is no different

00:01:45.471 --> 00:01:46.906
than what an athlete
would put themselves through?

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:49.642
-Absolutely. Absolutely.
But I still dance in my house.

00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:51.744
-You do?
Do you have, like,

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:53.479
the thickest carpet in the world
to make up for it?

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:54.814
[ Laughter ]

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:56.582
-Yes, because sometimes
when I fall,

00:01:56.582 --> 00:01:58.751
my husband, he just
bursts out laughing.

00:01:58.751 --> 00:02:01.854
-Does he like watching?
Is he happy when you dance?

00:02:01.854 --> 00:02:03.222
-Yeah.
-Alright, good.

00:02:03.222 --> 00:02:05.825
I mean I would be.
-I mean, of course.

00:02:05.825 --> 00:02:07.459
-When you danced on concrete,

00:02:07.459 --> 00:02:09.362
what were your early days of
hip-hop dancing?

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:13.199
Where were you doing it?
-Just, like, illegal nightclubs.

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:14.734
Ooh, can I say that?
-Yeah.

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:16.402
-Okay.
-I mean, I'm assuming

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:19.472
they're not going to file
a grievance against them now.

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:21.340
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.242
-Statute of limitations
probably went through.

00:02:23.242 --> 00:02:24.711
So, where were
the illegal nightclubs?

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:28.247
-Oh, it was...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:28.247 --> 00:02:31.317
-Oh, now you're clamming up,
Miss Perez.

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:33.419
I don't know...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:37.022
-Um, well, there was this
pop-up nightclub

00:02:37.022 --> 00:02:38.624
called Car Wash.
-Okay.

00:02:38.624 --> 00:02:40.926
-And, yeah,
so, they would just --

00:02:40.926 --> 00:02:43.963
Sometimes it would be
an abandoned warehouse.

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:46.765
Sometimes it would be
in a trucking service

00:02:46.765 --> 00:02:48.434
where they shouldn't have been.

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:51.670
-So you would come in
as a sort of entertainment

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:53.405
over the course of the night,
to dance the hip-hop?

00:02:53.405 --> 00:02:56.308
-What?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:56.308 --> 00:02:58.544
I wasn't the entertainment
for the night.

00:02:58.544 --> 00:02:59.745
-Oh, you were just
dancing to be part --

00:02:59.745 --> 00:03:01.614
-I was just dancing.
And also, at that time,

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:05.618
I was working for...
[ Laughter ]

00:03:05.618 --> 00:03:07.253
-By the way, when you said
you were dancing

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:08.888
at an abandoned truck
or whatever,

00:03:08.888 --> 00:03:12.091
I wasn't like, "What?!"
I just went with it.

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:13.559
I don't even know
what you're talking about.

00:03:13.559 --> 00:03:16.095
A trucking what?
Like a trucking depot?

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:17.930
Like a weigh station
off the highway?

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:19.966
What the -- Just rolled with it.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:21.834
I was like, "I'm not gonna
call out Rosie."

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:24.436
[ Laughter ]

00:03:24.436 --> 00:03:26.606
-Oh, my gosh. Oh, man.

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:30.009
No, I would go there to see --
I was also working

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:31.944
at "In Living Color"
during that time.

00:03:31.944 --> 00:03:35.214
Not only with the Fly Girls,
I had to do the dance,

00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:37.183
but I also booked
the musical acts.

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:39.151
So I would go
to these nightclubs

00:03:39.151 --> 00:03:40.953
to see what was the hot thing.

00:03:40.953 --> 00:03:43.389
And I would have to predict
what was hot

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:45.057
before their record
would come out,

00:03:45.057 --> 00:03:46.792
because, you know,
there's a delay.

00:03:46.792 --> 00:03:49.762
And so I would see, you know,
Leaders of the New School,

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:52.298
and that's where Busta Rhymes
came from,

00:03:52.298 --> 00:03:53.899
the Wu-Tang Clan.

00:03:53.899 --> 00:03:56.802
Oh, my God, I remember
when I met Ol' Dirty Bastard,

00:03:56.802 --> 00:03:58.604
he licked my hand.
It was so disgusting.

00:03:58.604 --> 00:04:00.339
[ Laughter ]
Oh, my gosh.

00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:04.043
-Aptly named.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:04.043 --> 00:04:05.544
-So, stuff like that.
So it was --

00:04:05.544 --> 00:04:07.413
-And was that something
that they -- I mean,

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:08.848
they must have had
a lot of faith

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:11.250
in your ability to see talent,
when you went back and said,

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.152
"Hey, these Wu-Tang Clan,
we got to have them."

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:15.421
Did they just sort of
take your word for it?

00:04:15.421 --> 00:04:17.990
-They said no to Wu-Tang.
They were a little too hardcore.

00:04:17.990 --> 00:04:20.627
-Yeah, well, I get it. For TV --
-Yeah, for TV.

00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:23.363
-Back then especially,
they were very...

00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:25.264
Is it amazing to you now,
like, as somebody

00:04:25.264 --> 00:04:30.069
who was dancing in that era,
that dance on the apps,

00:04:30.069 --> 00:04:32.204
like TikTok,
has become so modern

00:04:32.204 --> 00:04:33.672
because kids are watching
each other dance

00:04:33.672 --> 00:04:35.107
and learning new dance moves?

00:04:35.107 --> 00:04:37.076
Do you ever look
at dancing on TikTok?

00:04:37.076 --> 00:04:41.180
-Um, yes, because my sister,
Carmen, sends me TikTok videos

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.482
all throughout the day.
It's very irritating.

00:04:43.482 --> 00:04:44.984
-Uh-huh.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:47.353
-And you know,
some of them are asinine,

00:04:47.353 --> 00:04:50.089
but when it comes to
the dancing ones, I love them.

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:52.091
I just love 'em.
-Oh, that's good.

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:55.794
-Yeah. There's these two
older couples on TikTok.

00:04:55.794 --> 00:04:57.830
I think they're --
they're Latino,

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.265
but I don't know if they're
Dominican or Puerto Rican

00:04:59.265 --> 00:05:00.900
or Cuban, but
they're one of those things.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.903
And they -- they hump
on each other,

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:06.405
and they're senior citizens.
Have you seen it?

00:05:06.405 --> 00:05:10.476
It's amazing. It's amazing.
-What age are we talking about,

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:12.511
these people?
-I'm talking like 90.

00:05:12.511 --> 00:05:13.712
-And they're
humping on each other?

00:05:13.712 --> 00:05:15.013 size:81% position:59%
-And they're humping
on each other.

00:05:15.013 --> 00:05:16.449
-Oh, my God.
-Yeah. It's great.

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:18.384
-That's great. Good for them.
-Yeah.

00:05:18.384 --> 00:05:20.686
-You know, if I'm 90 and
I'm humping on somebody,

00:05:20.686 --> 00:05:22.889
I'm gonna put it online.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:26.492
I want everybody to see it.
You also -- You didn't just --

00:05:26.492 --> 00:05:28.794
-Sorry, that image
of you humping.

00:05:28.794 --> 00:05:31.430
-What's wrong?
It's not laughable.

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:34.099
It's sexy.
-Okay. Sorry.

00:05:34.099 --> 00:05:36.269
-You should be going,
"Ooh, ooh."

00:05:36.269 --> 00:05:37.703
[ Laughter ]

00:05:37.703 --> 00:05:40.639
Wait. You also ran a club
back in the day, right?

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:42.008
-I did.
-How'd it go?

00:05:42.008 --> 00:05:43.609
-It was horrible.
-Why?

00:05:43.609 --> 00:05:47.480
-Because I -- I thought
it was a genius idea.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:50.349
It was me and
my friend Bobbito Garcia.

00:05:50.349 --> 00:05:51.783
You know Bobbito?
-Yeah, well.

00:05:51.783 --> 00:05:53.118
-Who -- You do?

00:05:53.118 --> 00:05:54.821
-No, I don't know
Bobbito Garcia.

00:05:54.821 --> 00:05:56.355
[ Laughter ]

00:05:56.355 --> 00:05:59.258
Was he ever with a guy wearing
a plaid shirt like this?

00:05:59.258 --> 00:06:01.093
[ Laughter ]

00:06:01.093 --> 00:06:05.798
-Well, he -- That's when he
went by Bobbito the Barber,

00:06:05.798 --> 00:06:09.668
but now he goes by Bobbito
Kool Bob Love Cucumber Slice.

00:06:09.668 --> 00:06:11.137
-Okay.
-Okay.

00:06:11.137 --> 00:06:16.509
-So [Laughs] we were friends,
and he and this girl are like,

00:06:16.509 --> 00:06:17.709
"Do you want to
do a club together?"

00:06:17.709 --> 00:06:19.711
And I go, "Okay.
Oh, what should we name it?"

00:06:19.711 --> 00:06:21.180
I go, "Let's call it love.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:24.383
And how are we going to get
people to come?"

00:06:24.383 --> 00:06:26.885
Because everyone knew
Bobbito Garcia,

00:06:26.885 --> 00:06:31.090
and they knew me.
And so I said, "What if we say,

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:33.192
before 9:00, you could
get in for free

00:06:33.192 --> 00:06:35.628
if you make out,
like tongue make out,

00:06:35.628 --> 00:06:37.529
at the door?"
-So you make out

00:06:37.529 --> 00:06:39.598
in front of the bouncer,
and then he lets you in?

00:06:39.598 --> 00:06:42.701
-Yes, before 9:00.
There was a line down the block

00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:44.770
at 7:00 p.m.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:46.472
We made no money.
-Uh-huh.

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:48.574
-At all.
The other partner,

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:52.511
she was so pissed at me.
And then it was packed, right?

00:06:52.511 --> 00:06:56.015
We probably made like $1,000.
That's it.

00:06:56.015 --> 00:06:58.684
And the rent was $3,000.
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:58.684 --> 00:07:02.821
-And then somebody
called the fire department.

00:07:02.821 --> 00:07:06.091
The fire department
gave us a fine, shut us down,

00:07:06.091 --> 00:07:08.394
so we were in the hole.
It was -- It was terrible,

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:10.563
but it was great fun!
-For one night?

00:07:10.563 --> 00:07:12.064
-For one night,
it was off the hook.

00:07:12.064 --> 00:07:14.633
It was really great fun, yeah.
-So it sounds like maybe

00:07:14.633 --> 00:07:16.068
good at parties,
bad at business.

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:18.437
-Yes. Yes, yes.
-Hey, I want to ask you this.

00:07:18.437 --> 00:07:20.038
A lot of people, especially
during the pandemic,

00:07:20.038 --> 00:07:21.574
were saying,
"New York is over.

00:07:21.574 --> 00:07:24.076
This is never gonna come back
as a great city."

00:07:24.076 --> 00:07:26.145
How did you feel
during that time,

00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:28.080
when sort of this city,
during the pandemic,

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.016
emptied out?
-I thought that they weren't

00:07:30.016 --> 00:07:31.884
real New Yorkers.
-The people who left?

00:07:31.884 --> 00:07:33.285
There you go.
-Yes.

00:07:33.285 --> 00:07:35.054
Thank you very much for
that one person that clapped.

00:07:35.054 --> 00:07:36.889
[ Laughter ]

00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:39.492
Up on top, I see you. Holla.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:39.492 --> 00:07:42.728
Yeah, because it's like,
you know, during 9/11,

00:07:42.728 --> 00:07:44.964
people were leaving.
I stayed, you know.

00:07:44.964 --> 00:07:46.799
It's, like, we got to support
our city.

00:07:46.799 --> 00:07:49.201
And when the pandemic hit,
you know,

00:07:49.201 --> 00:07:51.203
we could have went
to Connecticut.

00:07:51.203 --> 00:07:52.972
We could have went
Upstate New York or Jersey

00:07:52.972 --> 00:07:54.507
to my daughter-in-law's house.
And I said,

00:07:54.507 --> 00:07:56.943
"No, we're gonna stick this out.
We're gonna stick it out."

00:07:56.943 --> 00:07:58.244
So, yeah.

00:07:58.244 --> 00:07:59.678
-Well, the city is lucky
to have you,

00:07:59.678 --> 00:08:01.013
and it has been
for a long time.

00:08:01.013 --> 00:08:03.015
You -- This show --
wonderful show,

00:08:03.015 --> 00:08:04.149
you're working
with Bryan Cranston.

00:08:04.149 --> 00:08:05.251
You filmed it
down in New Orleans.

00:08:05.251 --> 00:08:06.752
-Yeah.
-Did you enjoy your time

00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:08.320
in New Orleans?
Did you go and sightsee?

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:09.455
-No.
-Okay.

00:08:09.455 --> 00:08:10.656
-No.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:10.656 --> 00:08:12.357
-Not a sightseer in general?
-No, no, no.

00:08:12.357 --> 00:08:15.795
Actually, the only time
that I ever enjoyed sightseeing

00:08:15.795 --> 00:08:17.262
is when I was on
"The Flight Attendant,"

00:08:17.262 --> 00:08:20.299
and we went to Iceland.
-Uh-huh.

00:08:20.299 --> 00:08:22.001
-That was amazing.
-Iceland's amazing?

00:08:22.001 --> 00:08:24.536
-Yeah, because I like the cold,
and that's why New Orleans,

00:08:24.536 --> 00:08:25.971
I didn't leave the hotel.

00:08:25.971 --> 00:08:27.172
-Too hot?
-Too hot.

00:08:27.172 --> 00:08:28.540
I don't like the hot.
I don't like the heat.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.108
I don't like
being hot and sweaty.

00:08:30.108 --> 00:08:33.311
My hair goes poof.
It's just not a good look

00:08:33.311 --> 00:08:35.180
for me, you know.
But the people there were nice.

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:36.915
-Okay, so, you liked
the people of New Orleans.

00:08:36.915 --> 00:08:38.017
You just didn't
care for the heat.

00:08:38.017 --> 00:08:39.751
-I didn't care for the heat
at all.

00:08:39.751 --> 00:08:41.186
It's muggy. It's terrible.

00:08:41.186 --> 00:08:43.256
I remember, during one day
we were shooting,

00:08:43.256 --> 00:08:45.591
it was so hot,
I ripped off my Spanx

00:08:45.591 --> 00:08:48.127
right there on-set.
I couldn't take it.

00:08:48.127 --> 00:08:50.462
It was too -- It was horrible.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:50.462 --> 00:08:52.798
-You work with Bryan Cranston
on this.

00:08:52.798 --> 00:08:54.300
You play a prosecutor.
-Yes.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.535
-Were you excited
to play a prosecutor?

00:08:56.535 --> 00:08:58.537
-Yes, I was, I was.
-That's good.

00:08:58.537 --> 00:09:01.874
-I know a lot of D.A.s
and assistant D.A.s,

00:09:01.874 --> 00:09:04.576
and a lot of law enforcement
are in my family.

00:09:04.576 --> 00:09:06.479
My daughter-in-law
is in law enforcement.

00:09:06.479 --> 00:09:09.214
You know, I just --
I just love it.

00:09:09.214 --> 00:09:14.519
I loved it, and I really --
I really relied on them

00:09:14.519 --> 00:09:16.121
to create this character.

00:09:16.121 --> 00:09:18.256
And I only had two days
to create the character.

00:09:18.256 --> 00:09:22.628
I got a call on Friday,
had a shoot on Sunday or Monday.

00:09:22.628 --> 00:09:26.498
It was -- It was nerve-racking,
And so I was on the phone

00:09:26.498 --> 00:09:29.035
on the way to the airport with
my daughter-in-law,

00:09:29.035 --> 00:09:30.869
with my two assistant D.A.s.

00:09:30.869 --> 00:09:32.471
I don't want to
name their names.

00:09:32.471 --> 00:09:35.908
You know, and even when --
right before set,

00:09:35.908 --> 00:09:38.510
I'm just making notes and, like,
being all, like, nervous.

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:41.380
And when I got there,
I sat down.

00:09:41.380 --> 00:09:42.982
I was a nervous wreck,
and I just --

00:09:42.982 --> 00:09:45.651
I just let her come out, Olivia,

00:09:45.651 --> 00:09:48.020
and she came out,
like, really pretty badass.

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:53.292
And so I got a call
when it premiered last night.

00:09:53.292 --> 00:09:55.094
They were like, "You did good."
-Alright.

00:09:55.094 --> 00:09:57.363
-Like, "Yay!"
-Well, I agree. I second that.

00:09:57.363 --> 00:10:01.667
And may I just say I find you
pretty badass in real life,

00:10:01.667 --> 00:10:03.603
as well.
It is so nice having you back.

00:10:03.603 --> 00:10:04.703
-Thank you.
-Please come back soon.

00:10:04.703 --> 00:10:06.771
You guys, give it up
for Rosie Perez.

00:10:06.771 --> 00:10:08.440
[ Cheers and applause ]
New episodes of "Your Honor"

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:10.576
debut Fridays on streaming
and on demand,

00:10:10.576 --> 00:10:12.077
and airs Sunday nights
on Showtime.

00:10:12.077 --> 00:10:13.579
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night.""

